WEBVTT

00:00:00.016 --> 00:00:07.157
PostgreSQL offers USING as a convenient alternative to ON when both tables share the same join-key name.

00:00:07.157 --> 00:00:12.742
If patients and admissions both contain patient_id, then USING patient_id tells PostgreSQL to match those

00:00:12.742 --> 00:00:18.362
columns and return one copy of the join key in the output.

00:00:18.362 --> 00:00:25.877
The result relationship is the same as if we had written an ON condition that compared the two patient_id

00:00:25.877 --> 00:00:31.047
columns directly. USING can make simple joins shorter and cleaner, but ON is more flexible.

00:00:31.047 --> 00:00:35.352
Use ON when the key names are different, when you need multiple conditions,

00:00:35.352 --> 00:00:39.882
or when the relationship requires an expression.

00:00:39.882 --> 00:00:45.457
For example, if one table uses customer_id and another uses client_id, USING cannot express that relationship.

00:00:45.457 --> 00:00:51.937
If you need to match by both account_id and date, ON is clearer.

00:00:51.937 --> 00:00:55.572
The goal is not to use the shortest syntax possible.

00:00:55.572 --> 00:00:59.237
The goal is to make the relationship obvious.

00:00:59.237 --> 00:01:04.277
Whatever style you choose, you should still be able to explain the match in one sentence.
